    11	/*
    12	 * The level hash consists of hierarchical levels of arrays of pointers.
    13	 * The pointers may point to another level, a bucket, or NULL.
    14	 * The levels and buckets must be allocated in manner alike posix_memalign()
    15	 * to bookkeep additional information in pointer low bits.
    16	 *
    17	 * A level is an array of pointers.  Its size is a power of 2.  Levels
    18	 * may be different sizes, but on the same level the sizes are the same.
    19	 * Level sizes are specified by number of bits per level in lvlhsh->shift
    20	 * array.  A hash may have up to 7 levels.  There are two predefined
    21	 * shift arrays given by the first two shift array values:
    22	 *
    23	 * 1) [0, 0]:  [4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4] on a 64-bit platform or
    24	 *             [5, 5, 5, 5, 5, 5, 0] on a 32-bit platform,
    25	 *    so default size of levels is 128 bytes.
    26	 *
    27	 * 2) [0, 10]: [10, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 0] on a 64-bit platform or
    28	 *             [10, 5, 5, 5, 5, 0, 0] on a 32-bit platform,
    29	 *    so default size of levels is 128 bytes on all levels except
    30	 *    the first level.  The first level is 8K or 4K on 64-bit or 32-bit
    31	 *    platforms respectively.
    32	 *
    33	 * All buckets in a hash are the same size which is a power of 2.
    34	 * A bucket contains several entries stored and tested sequentially.
    35	 * The bucket size should be one or two CPU cache line size, a minimum
    36	 * allowed size is 32 bytes.  A default 128-byte bucket contains 10 64-bit
    37	 * entries or 15 32-bit entries.  Each entry consists of pointer to value
    38	 * data and 32-bit key.  If an entry value pointer is NULL, the entry is free.
    39	 * On a 64-bit platform entry value pointers are no aligned, therefore they
    40	 * are accessed as two 32-bit integers.  The rest trailing space in a bucket
    41	 * is used as pointer to next bucket and this pointer is always aligned.
    42	 * Although the level hash allows to store a lot of values in a bucket chain,
    43	 * this is non optimal way.  The large data set should be stored using
    44	 * several levels.
    45	 */
